.Dd April 12, 2026
.Dt HEXII 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hexii
.Nd a compact binary representation mixing hexadecimal and ASCII
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm hexii
.Op Fl aAeEhHqsSvxX
.Op Fl c Ar number
.Ar file...
.Nm hexii
.Op Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility displays files in HexII format.

.Pp
.Bd -literal -offset indent
HexII is a mixture of ASCII and Hex
because ASCII dump is usually useless,
unless there is an ASCII string,
in which case the HEX part is useless.
.Ed
.Pp
Traditional hex dumps often waste space displaying ASCII representations of
binary data, while ASCII dumps are useless for non-printable content. HexII
intelligently interleaves both representations based on content, showing ASCII
text where useful and hexadecimal values where necessary to maximize
readability and minimize output clutter.
.Pp
The end-of-file is indicated by a solitary closing bracket `]', as it may not be
present.
.Sh OPTIONS
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Enable ANSI color output for syntax highlighting. This is the default behavior
and provides colored output for addresses, hex values, and a selection of
character cells to improve readability in terminals that support color.
.It Fl A
Disable ANSI color output, producing plain monochrome text suitable for piping
to other utilities or redirecting to files without color escape sequences.
.It Fl c Ar number
Format the output to display
.Ar number
columns (bytes) per row. The value must be between 0 and the maximum column
limit. The maximum column limit is a compile-time value, and is typically 4096.
The default is 16 columns per row.
.It Fl e
Enable C-style escape-character output. When enabled, control characters are
displayed as escaped sequences (e.g.,
.Sy \en
for newline,
.Sy \et
for tab) rather than their hexadecimal representation, making control character
sequences more human-readable.
.It Fl E
Disable C-style escape-character output, the default behavior. Control
characters and non-printable bytes are displayed in hexadecimal format.
.It Fl h
Enable hexadecimal display mode for printable characters. When enabled, all
printable characters are displayed as their hexadecimal byte values instead of
ASCII representation, providing a pure hex dump output while still maintaining
the HexII format structure.
.It Fl H
Disable hexadecimal display mode for printable characters, the default.
Printable ASCII characters are displayed as readable text (prefixed with a dot
for visual distinction) to improve readability.
.It Fl q
Enable quiet mode, disabling verbose output and setting the tool to its most
concise display state. This is the default behavior. This option reverses the
effect of the
.Fl v
option.
.It Fl s
Squash blank lines (rows of all zero bytes) from the output. When enabled,
consecutive blank lines are completely omitted, significantly reducing output
size for files with large blocks of zeros or uninitialized memory regions. This
is the default behavior.
.It Fl S
Suppress blank line squashing, preserving all rows in the output including
those containing only zero bytes. Use this when you need to see every byte
location or maintain accurate address continuity for all file offsets.
.It Fl v
Enable verbose output mode. In verbose mode, zero bytes are displayed as
.Sy 00
(in color) instead of blank space, and 0xFF bytes are displayed as
.Sy FF
instead of
.Sy ## ,
providing explicit visual representation of special byte values.
.It Fl V
Display version information and exit.
.It Fl x
Use lowercase hexadecimal digits (a-f) in all hexadecimal output, including
addresses and byte values.
.It Fl X
Use uppercase hexadecimal digits (A-F) in all hexadecimal output. This is the
default behavior.
.El
.Sh BEHAVIOR
By default,
.Nm
combines several display options:
.Bl -bullet -width Ds
.It
ANSI colors are enabled for improved readability
.It
Blank lines (all zero bytes) are squashed to reduce clutter
.It
Printable ASCII characters are shown as text with a leading dot
.It
Non-printable bytes are shown in hexadecimal
.It
Addresses and hex values use uppercase letters
.El
.Pp
Option flags can be combined freely. For example,
.Sy -asvx
enables ANSI color, squashes blank lines, enables verbose mode, and uses
lowercase hex.
.Sh PHILOSOPHY
.Nm
follows a minimalist design philosophy in both its output format specification
and implementation:
.Pp
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"Show what matters. Hide what doesn't."
.Ed
.Sh EXAMPLES
Display a binary file with default settings:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hexii myfile.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Show a file in pure unsquashed hex format without ANSI colors:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hexii -AhSv myfile.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Display a file with verbose output and escape sequences for control characters:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hexii -ve myfile.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Examine with 32 columns per row and lowercase hex digits:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hexii -c 32 -x myfile.bin
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits with status 0 on successful completion. If an error occurs while
reading a file, it exits with status greater than 0 and prints an error message
to standard error.
.Sh ATTRIBUTION
The HexII format was originally conceived and implemented by
.An Ange Albertini
in the
.Lk https://github.com/gunmetalbackupgooglecode/corkami corkami
project.
This C implementation is a modernized and extended version of the original
Python concept.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr hexdump 1 ,
.Xr od 1 ,
.Xr xxd 1
